  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "triads-applied-harmony-and-colour",
      "title": "Triads — Applied Harmony and Colour",
      "level": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "**Track:** theory | **Tier:** advanced | **Difficulty:** 6/10\n**Prerequisites:** Triads — Applied\n**Tags:** triads, extensions, jazz"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "overview",
      "title": "Overview",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Beyond three-note triads: **seventh chords**, **extensions**, and jazz colour — still grounded in triad stacks."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "seventh-chords",
      "title": "Seventh chords",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Add a seventh above the triad: major 7, dominant 7, minor 7, half-diminished. Dominant 7 (V7) drives cadences."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "extensions",
      "title": "Extensions",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "9, 11, 13 — tertian stacks. In jazz and some folk fusion, upper extensions add colour without changing the underlying function."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "representative-depth",
      "title": "Representative depth",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "You do not need every extension in every style. Irish session backup is often triadic; jazz standards use sevenths extensively — revisit in style units."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
